# THE LAYERS WHOSE REMAINDERS MUST MOVE TOGETHER, DERIVED. One row per ordered pair reachable from
# each other through parts, plus the reflexive pair for each member.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ `F⁺ ∩ (F⁺)ᵀ`, WHICH IS THE MODEL'S FOURTH FALSIFIER MADE COMPUTABLE. `pm:Layer` states it:
#    *if two candidate layers' remainders always move together, they were never two layers.* If A
#    is composed from B while B is composed from A there is no perturbation of one that leaves the
#    other fixed, so the pair fails the definition of a layer: the two cells are one cell.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND IT IS A RANK DEFICIENCY, WHICH IS THE SAME DEFECT IN THE OTHER ALGEBRA. Composition is
#    the linear map `x = (F Phi - E) x`, and two composed layers on a loop have linearly DEPENDENT
#    rows in it. Ordinal rank fails to exist and matrix rank falls at the same moment, for the same
#    reason. rank/evaluation_order.sqlc is the first half of that sentence.
#
# ⭐ ONE SELF-JOIN, NOT A SECOND WALK. composition/descent.sqlc is already the transitive closure,
#   so intersecting it with its converse gives an equivalence, and the classes fall out of a window
#   because transitivity guarantees every member sees the same set.
#
# ⛔ EMPTY IS THE GOOD OUTCOME AND IT IS ALSO WHAT AN UNREACHED RELATION RETURNS. It is empty on
#   this corpus. Probe it: close a loop across two documents inside a transaction and roll back,
#   and watch both instruments move.
#
# ⚠️ IT DOES NOT REPLACE THE FILER'S ATTESTATION. `pm:Stack` requires `couplings` because nobody
#   could check independence from the data; this means the corpus can now CONTRADICT the answer,
#   which is strictly more than it could do before and strictly less than deciding it.
-- composition/descent.sqlc intersected with its own converse; the classes of F+ ∩ (F+)ᵀ.
--
-- ⛔ THE WINDOW RUNS BEFORE `DISTINCT`, WHICH COST A WRONG NUMBER. Written as one SELECT DISTINCT
--   with `count(*) OVER` beside it, `members` counted DESCENT ROWS and not layers, so a class of
--   two reported five and the rule's detail said so in words. The dedup has to happen in a
--   subquery and the window has to sit outside it.
SELECT p.filing, p.layer, p.co_moves_with_filing, p.co_moves_with_layer,
       min(p.co_moves_with_filing || '/' || p.co_moves_with_layer) OVER w AS class,
       count(*) OVER w                                                    AS members
FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT a.root_filing AS filing, a.root_layer AS layer,
           a.filing AS co_moves_with_filing, a.layer AS co_moves_with_layer
    FROM      (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) a
    JOIN      (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) b ON  b.root_filing = a.filing      AND b.root_layer = a.layer
        AND b.filing      = a.root_filing AND b.layer      = a.root_layer
) p
WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY p.filing, p.layer)
